Z||000000||Ralfy9862||12-26-2000||05:10 PM||||Could you tell me exactly how you did your cherry bomb exhaust||||reg||1
Z||000001||william_thorniii||12-27-2000||10:13 AM||||I can reply on behalf of our simeon brother.  The converter was removed and a cherry bomb was inserted in its place.  The car was then required to pass the OH e-check sniffer test.  It passed with flying colors.  Any decent muffler shop can do the work... it is just a matter of finding one that will.  Take a specific interest in the type of cherry bomb that you are buying... some are manufactured of better materials than others.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000002||gtsonly||12-27-2000||02:08 PM||||but is the muffler left in place? glass pack replaces the cat with a muffler in the normal spot<P>------------------<BR>Chris Hatch <BR>1986 GT w/T-TOPs||||reg||5
Z||000003||william_thorniii||12-27-2000||02:17 PM||||Gtsonly,<P>Yes... the exhaust was not touched from the converter back.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000004||Philphine||12-27-2000||03:07 PM||||why have both? curious. dose the cherry bomb look like a cat for inspection purposes? sound? works like a resonator? no restriction worries from being routed through two mufflers? wussup wit dat?||||reg||1
Z||000005||fiero56||12-27-2000||03:15 PM||||Iv'e heard the exhaust on that '85GT(even drove it), and it is one of the best sounding exhausts I have ever heard from a six. It burbles more than the factory exhaust, is fairly mellow sounding at idle, but once you punch the throttle, it is completely different.||||reg||1
Z||000006||william_thorniii||12-28-2000||08:54 AM||||Phil,<P>I do not like loud raspy exhausts.  There is nothing worse than hearing many Saturns/Cavaliers/Hondas/Toyotas running around here.  The V6, as much as I love it, is not a powerful engine and does not sound as such.  The goal was to get a better and more throaty exhaust note than factory but not obnoxious.  I also own a 1956 Ford Fairlane with dual cherry bombs on virtually otherwise straight pipes.  I wanted a sound similar to my old 292, quiet at idle but with a nice burble yet almost raucous when up towards redline.  I had heard a car with the cat replaced with a straight pipe and was not happy with the results.  Much too harsh for my tastes.  Like 56 said, at idle the car sounds similar to stock.  I think that I acheived what I was trying to accomplish.  I had never thought about the visual inspection consideration.  In OH the cars are tested on a chassis dyno and the Fiero's ground clearance is too short for an adequate inspection of components underneath.  <P>William T. Thorn, III||||reg||1
Z||000007||Monkeyman||12-28-2000||08:18 PM||||I think the car sounds great.  Just like WTIII said, it's basically stock sounding at idle, but just wind the thing up!  It sounds much more like a V8 then.  I wouldn't mind it if it was slightly louder but this is really nice.  Whoop!  Whoop!||||reg||1
Z||000008||DaRkLoRD||12-29-2000||02:22 AM||||And it passed emissions??<P>this would be cool, I already love the way the 2.8 sounds at around 4,000 RPM.. if there's a chance I can make it sound even better, without increasing the car's pollution.... I'm all for it!<P>------------------<BR>  <A HREF="mailto:steve@fieroproject.com">steve@fieroproject.com</A>  <BR><A HREF="http://www.fieroproject.com" TARGET=_blank>http://www.fieroproject.com</A>||||reg||14
Z||000009||Ralfy9862||12-29-2000||01:19 PM||||So what your saying is that you have a cherry bomb cat, or that you put another straight through muffler in the  place of the cat, wouldnt that restrict the exhaust? <BR>do they make cats that would make the exhaust loader<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000010||jerpro||12-30-2000||09:48 AM||||ralfy...<BR>a very simple way (but not advised!!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">) is to drill a small hole about 1/16th of an inch in the muffler. this will still keep the car quiet but rumble when revved.<BR>I think this will work because it is what was happening to my old dodge caravan.<BR>If you are concerned about inspections, you can drill the hole on the top of the muffler so it is virtually invisible to the eye and will not rust as easy in salty conditions.<P>Just a cheap suggestion, I have not done it on my fiero and have no idea if it will work or not.<BR>MAybe someone else can give a little more input on the matter...<BR><P>------------------<BR>Only try your best, and if you don't succeed... TRY HARDER.||||reg||1
Z||000011||Philphine||12-30-2000||10:12 AM||||i have a free flow cat with stock gt exhaust, but it's just on a 4cyl. also had some big (5") resonator tips added. since i had it all done at the same time i don't know what might be most responsible for the exhaust note change. it's a little deeper but i guess with a four there's not much to listen for anyway. actually as far as "listening" the sound i like best is opening the sunroof and hearing my intake through the indy scoop. but again, something bigger that would be sucking in more air and revving higher would be more impressive still.<P>my main concern and the reason i wanted the exhaust change anyway (besides apperance and needing a cat) is for a swap. how big of an engine do you think this exhaust system would support without being a performance hinderance itself?<P> ||||reg||1
Z||000012||bubbajoexx||12-31-2000||11:11 AM||||use the z24 cat, it is 2 1/4" in and 3" out it will give you better exhaust flow and great sound.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Philphine||12-31-2000||02:34 PM||||bubba,  just described my current set up above. if i went with your swap will this do me ok?||||reg||1
